"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,340\nEpisode 4\n\n3\n00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:10,000\nStereotype threat, carefully\n\n4\n00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:24,378\nIn 1995, a famous experiment suggested that simply framing a test as a measure of ability could lower the scores of students whose group is stereotyped as weaker.\n\n5\n00:00:24,974 --> 00:00:27,096\nIt was called stereotype threat,\n\n6\n00:00:27,523 --> 00:00:30,973\nand it became one of the best known ideas in education.\n\n7\n00:00:31,143 --> 00:00:32,842\nThe idea was intuitive.\n\n8\n00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:37,801\nWorry about confirming a stereotype takes up attention and working memory,\n\n9\n00:00:38,071 --> 00:00:40,530\nthe same resources the test needs.\n\n10\n00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,162\nBut later work tells a more careful story.\n\n11\n00:00:43,661 --> 00:00:51,138\nA 2015 meta-analysis of studies with schoolgirls on math and science tests found a small average effect,\n\n12\n00:00:51,448 --> 00:00:55,482\nabout point two, with clear signs of publication bias.\n\n13\n00:00:55,958 --> 00:01:02,319\nAnd a set of three studies with nine hundred and thirty one students found no evidence of the effect at all.\n\n14\n00:01:02,489 --> 00:01:09,702\nA 2019 meta-analysis of two hundred and twelve adult samples also found signs of bias.\n\n15\n00:01:10,116 --> 00:01:13,201\nIn settings most like real high stakes tests,\n\n16\n00:01:13,498 --> 00:01:17,073\nthe effects ranged from zero to about point one four.\n\n17\n00:01:17,243 --> 00:01:21,253\nSo stereotype threat may be real in some situations.\n\n18\n00:01:21,682 --> 00:01:25,690\nBut it is probably smaller and narrower than the headlines suggested,\n\n19\n00:01:26,024 --> 00:01:29,989\nand it is unlikely to explain most achievement gaps on its own.\n\n20\n00:01:30,159 --> 00:01:31,266\nWhy still care?\n\n21\n00:01:31,785 --> 00:01:34,031\nBecause the conditions that might trigger it,\n\n22\n00:01:34,044 --> 00:01:36,751\nlike being the only one of your group in the room,\n\n23\n00:01:36,929 --> 00:01:39,853\nor hearing that a test measures fixed ability,\n\n24\n00:01:39,957 --> 00:01:41,682\nare worth avoiding anyway.\n\n25\n00:01:42,105 --> 00:01:44,163\nAnd they cost nothing to change.\n\n26\n00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:45,049\nFor teaching.\n\n27\n00:01:45,443 --> 00:01:48,600\nDon't describe tests as measures of natural ability.\n\n28\n00:01:48,993 --> 00:01:52,549\nAvoid collecting demographic questions right before an exam.\n\n29\n00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,865\nTry not to leave any student as the only member of their group on a team.\n\n30\n00:01:57,149 --> 00:01:58,506\nAnd keep the bar high.\n\n31\n00:01:59,031 --> 00:02:01,924\nLower the stakes of identity,\n\n32\n00:02:02,264 --> 00:02:04,208\nnot the standards.\n\n33\n00:02:04,386 --> 00:02:05,751\nOne tip to share with students: focus on the task,\n\n34\n00:02:05,856 --> 00:02:07,415\nnot on representing a group.\n\n35\n00:02:07,585 --> 00:02:09,043\nTake the threat seriously.\n\n36\n00:02:09,244 --> 00:02:11,313\nTake the evidence seriously too.\n\n"
